/*!

\page cartesiangridplugin_wizard_page Cartesian Grid Wizard

The wizard does in a single dialog everything needed to go from a geometry to a Cartesian MED file: create or update the
mesh, set the grid, write the file, and optionally compute the mesh. You don't need to go through the <b>Create Mesh</b>
dialog first. Open it with <b>Mesh > SMESH plugins > Cartesian Grid Wizard</b>.

\image html cartesiangrid_wizard.png "Cartesian Grid Wizard"

<h2>The fields of the dialog</h2>

- \b Geometry: the shape to mesh. While the selection button next to the field is pressed, the field follows what you
  select in the Object Browser (a GEOM or SHAPER shape). The dialog does not block the rest of SALOME, so you can select
  objects while it is open.
- \b Mesh: the list shows the existing meshes of this geometry that use the Cartesian Grid 3D algorithm, plus
  <i>New mesh</i>.
  - If you choose an existing mesh, the wizard updates it. If that mesh has the algorithm but no hypothesis yet, the
    wizard adds a Cartesian Grid Parameters hypothesis to it.
  - If you choose <i>New mesh</i>, the wizard creates a mesh with the algorithm and the hypothesis. This mesh stays in
    the study as an ordinary mesh, that you can open and edit later like any other.
- \b Grid: the same tabs as in the \ref cartesiangridplugin_hypo_page "hypothesis dialog", one per axis. They show the
  grid of the chosen mesh.
- \b Export: the name of the MED file to write, and how the zones of the file are chosen:
  - <i>Geometry groups named</i> a prefix (<code>MAT_</code> by default): each geometry group whose name starts with
    this prefix becomes a zone;
  - <i>Solids, one zone per solid</i>: each solid becomes a zone, named after the solid.

  See \ref cartesiangridplugin_export_page "Cartesian MED export" for details.
- <b>Also compute the SMESH mesh</b> (optional): also builds the hexahedra in SMESH, and creates one mesh group per
  geometry group of solids. A group is skipped if its name is already used by a mesh group, or if it holds faces or
  edges instead of solids. Building the SMESH mesh is only needed if you want to look at the cells: an SMESH mesh
  stores every node and every hexahedron, so it is much bigger and slower to create than the Cartesian MED file.

<h2>Running</h2>

\b Run first writes the MED file, then computes the mesh if you asked for it. A progress bar shows the progress, and the
\b Cancel button stops the run. The MED file does not need the computed mesh, so if the compute fails or is canceled,
the MED file is kept and the summary shows a warning.

The dialog stays open after a run, so you can change a setting and run again. Note that if you change the grid of a
mesh that was already computed, its cells are cleared until it is computed again.

At the end of a run, a summary shows what was done, in two parts:
- <b>SMESH mesh</b> (only when the mesh was computed): the number of hexahedra, the mesh groups created from the
  geometry groups and those that were skipped, and the compute warnings;
- <b>MED file</b>: the file name, how the zones were chosen, the size of the grid, the number of cells in each zone
  and in no zone, and notes about the zone names (see
  \ref cartesiangridplugin_export_solids "One zone per solid").

If anything went wrong, the summary is shown as a warning.

\b Help opens this page.

*/
